{
  "id": "Compound::DB01567 - Gene::2563",
  "verbalization": "Fludiazepam, a benzodiazepine, is shown to bind to Gabrr3, Gabrr2, and Gabrg1, all of which are subunits of the Gaba-a receptor. These receptors are critical for the modulation of neuronal activity, and their participation in Gaba-a receptor activity is a well-documented biological process. The consistent binding of Fludiazepam to these related Gaba-a receptor subunits suggests a potential mechanism of action involving the modulation of GABAergic signaling. The high scores from the three paths indicate a strong, reliable interaction between Fludiazepam and Gabrd, as it is directly linked to the participation in Gaba-a receptor activity. This relationship is supported by the shared functional role of these subunits in the same receptor complex, reinforcing the likelihood of a mechanistic link. The evidence suggests that Fludiazepam may influence GABAergic signaling through interaction with these receptor subunits, potentially modulating neuronal activity.",
